ee coverage is probably incomplete (group `proto-free-maps`, read).** `src/html/client.js:144` (`hunkClosed = {}`) and `:900` (`counts = {}`) are plain objects. I did not confirm what keys they take. If either is keyed by a user-supplied string, it has the same flaw the group claims to fix. Check both, or narrow the intent to the sites actually changed.\n\n**Misgraded necessity**\n\n4. **`note-limits` is labelled `required`, but parts are defensive (read).** The caps on `reasons`, `alternatives` and `risks` are justified by the author's own threat-model comment (\"notes come from anyone who can push\"), not by reproduced failures in every case. The reproduced crash was `risks` (`show` stack overflow) and the quadratic page on groups. The `claimsProblem` check on the add side is a design choice. `required` is defensible under \"fix what was reproduced\", but I would split out the add-side cap and the alternatives/reasons caps as `defensive`.\n\n5. **`audit-tests` `supporting` is fine (read).** Its hunk is test-only, and it serves the groups the TODO names. Not misgraded.\n\n6. **`terminal-clean` mixes a move with new behaviour (read).** `plain()` moves from `reviews.ts` to `plain.ts` and is imported at about 10 sites. That is mechanical. Only `readNote`'s `cleanStrings` and the file-name print sites are real changes. Label the move `mechanical` so reviewers see the behaviour change separately.\n\n**Removal candidates**\n\n7. **`cleanStrings` at read plus `plain()` at print sites (group `terminal-clean`, read).** Gloss text is now cleaned in `readNote`, and file names and subjects are cleaned at print. This is not redundant for file names, but for gloss text the author accepts a tradeoff: adopt and carry write cleaned text into notes, and review stamps for glosses containing CR, C1 or bidi characters change. That is already listed as a risk. If you want less blast radius, clean only at print sites (the author rejected this as too many sites). Impact of the current choice: stamp-key drift for a small class of glosses.\n\n**Simplifications**\n\n8. **`readReviews` skip counting (group `review-cap`, read).** The loop with `skipped` and `isDecisionLine` is correct. A cleaner form is a `filter` that collects the skipped count, but the saving is small. Not worth changing.\n\n**Missing risks**\n\n9. **Markdown `**` scan is still unbounded (group `markdown-bound`, read).** Only the single-`*` emphasis scan and the link regex are bounded. The strong-emphasis branch still does `s.indexOf('**', i + 2)`, which scans to end of text when no closer exists. Text made of many `**` openers with no closing `**` may still be quadratic. I did not run a timing test. The test \"markdown that is quadratic to scan\" only covers `*a `, `[` and `[a](x`, so it would not catch this. Add a `**`-only case to the test, or bound the strong scan the same way.\n\n10. **Refused glosses fail the whole range (group `note-limits`, read).** The author lists this as a follow-up, which is fair. Note that `loadGloss` now throws for any commit in a range, so one hostile commit in a range breaks `html` and `show` for all of them. This is the most user-visible residual risk.\n\n11. **Tests were not run in this review (read).** I checked the TODO diff and the code paths by reading and one `git show`. I did not run `test/untrusted.test.ts`, the browser tests, or a timing check. The `verified` label applies only to finding 1.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
