de the existing `flatMap` callback. For example, start the callback with `if (line.length > MAX_REVIEW_LINE) { if (!isDecisionLine(line)) skipped++; return [] }`. That removes the `kept` array, the `for` loop and the `let skipped` split across two blocks. The `findings.length > MAX_FINDINGS` check can sit beside it.\n   - **Why:** There is one pass and one place where skips are counted.\n   - **Size:** about 8 lines.\n\n3. **The \"old → new\" file label is built in three places (`terminal-clean`)**\n   - **Suggestion:** `hunkLine` in `review.ts` and `hunkLabel` in `render.ts` both build `h.oldFile && h.oldFile !== h.file ? \\`${h.oldFile} → ${h.file}\\` : h.file` and wrap it in `plain(...)`. Add a `fileLabel(h)` to `render.ts` that does this and use it in both. Any other print site that does the same, such as `why`, could use it too.\n   - **Why:** The cleaning rule for file names lives in one function. The next print site is less likely to forget `plain`, which is the exact bug class this audit is fixing.\n   - **Size:** small.\n\n4. **The em-scan limit is written out three times (`markdown-bound`, `client.js` h27)**\n   - **Suggestion:** Add `var end = Math.min(s.length, i + MAX_SPAN)` before the loop. Then use `for (j = i + 2; j < end; j++)` and `if (j < end)`. The current `j < s.length && j < i + MAX_SPAN` appears in both the loop and the post-check. The loop also needs the second check only because it can stop at either bound.\n   - **Why:** There is one bound, and the loop condition and the success check can't drift apart.\n   - **Size:** 3 lines.\n\n5. **`checkTracking` is only called by `fetchTracking` (`sync-tracking`, `sync.ts` h16)**\n   - **Suggestion:** Inline `checkTracking` into `fetchTracking`. Also consider making `MAX_GROUPS` and `MAX_ITEMS` one constant, since both are 500 and both guard the same hostile-list-size concern.\n   - **Why:** It removes one function and one concept from a 14-line addition.\n   - **Size:** very small.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES