jor, **verified**)\n`src/schema.ts:92` limits each group to 500 hunk references (`.max(MAX_ITEMS)`). A one-group gloss of a change that touches more than 500 hunks is a normal case, such as a rename across 600 files.\n- **Steps:** I made a commit that changes 600 files, then glossed it with the previous build (HEAD~1) using one group that lists `h1`…`h600`. That build accepted it: \"glossed …: 1 groups over 600 hunks\".\n- **Result with the new build:** `show` and `html` both fail with \"the gloss on 954967bb0a is malformed; replace it with `git gloss add --force`\".\n- **The suggested fix fails too:** the new `add` refuses the same annotation with \"Too big: expected array to have <=500 items → at groups[0].hunks\".\n\nSo existing valid notes become unreadable, and the error message points to a fix that doesn't work. Because a refused gloss fails the whole command, one such commit also breaks `html` or `show` for any range that contains it.\n\n### 2. `add` accepts a gloss that its own readers then refuse (minor, **verified**)\nThe claim-factor check in `src/loader.ts:98` only runs when a gloss is read; `add` never applies it. It can also catch real glosses.\n- **Steps:** I made a commit that rewrites a 1,000-line file (2,000 changed lines in one hunk) and glossed it with 7 groups that each claim `h1`, for example one group per dependency in a lockfile bump.\n- **Result:** `add` succeeds (\"glossed 02b86d5c20: 7 groups over 1 hunks\"). `show` then refuses it: \"claims 14000 changed lines across its groups for a diff of 2000; it is refused as malformed\". The limit there is 5 × 2,000 + 1,000 = 11,000.\n\nThe user gets no warning when they write the gloss, and the error tells them to rerun `add --force`, which would write the same thing again. `add` should run the same check, or the limit should not count several groups sharing one hunk this heavily.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
