ce.\n\n**Simplifications**\n\n9. `cleanStrings` in `git.ts` is a recursive walk over the whole annotation. If the only printing sites are the ones already wrapped in `plain()`, a shorter alternative is to clean at print time. The author has chosen read-time cleaning deliberately, and the write-back consequence is listed as a risk, so I would leave it.\n\n**Missing risks**\n\n10. **Gloss-level lock-in of cleaned text (read).** `readNote` returns cleaned strings. `stamp` and `glossKeys` run on the cleaned annotation, so a review recorded against raw text could show \"gloss changed\" after cleaning. Confirm that `glossKeys` only uses ids and hunks, or record it.\n\n11. **Verification of the test-first claim is not possible from this snapshot (read).** The summary says each fix was seen failing first. The repo has one snapshot commit, so there is no history to show it.\n\n**Verified**\n\n- `node --test test/untrusted.test.ts`: 17 of 17 pass, including the two browser tests, which ran against the clone's bin.\n- The `ary` fix is confirmed by the `BadRequest` class (`server.ts:11`) and the try wrap (`server.ts:129`).\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
