95b7f","f908fd3480b3bd90","f90c9c528b909b7a","f98b43ebecaf00f5","f99635e5ae93019d","fa45288914e1d057","fa6cf77c3fc9bc71","fa9cc80d0edf1a89","faba1db88865050d","fb1cbf85e9ab46e6","fb3e8463219b209c","fb9c353a9fb798c5","fbaa94468ed9d68c","fbbdf262540e701f","fc3557b93fc7c9ae","fcc98c8ff56b619c","fcdd2016a2868e87","fcfe8f1cfb9b51f1","fd12ccc8a40d39dd","fdb0db069071c03e","fde9ac9123ff02c4","fe0700eaaecf5835","ff1a92e8f54f7b81","ff1eee77e340be01","ff20ab4224c80a1f","ff2cb4c5e68ef08d","ff606946a11f00ea","ff9a03912482424b","ffcd83bb62d58d2c"],"groups":["3642450da2847844","296609ebf23dcffb","9d79a0dc87ab4a66","9a7204ca6170dd30","05a5da09f2d455ca","e1c5cd8fdf7700a5","347372086fc95f48","2ddc0989c67b0f29","e18a720f10a1179b","031ba96ad8075139","1c0841d71fc3e776","274054a5e5fa26be","fb3839647bc4c055","db65dd826ca4cfe9","de6dd1d783525300","dc0e68db7919361a"],"findings":"I found two defects worth fixing and one minor gap. The 17 tests in `test/untrusted.test.ts` all pass, including the browser ones; none of them covers the cases below.\n\n### 1. Major: the 256 KiB review cap hides real reviews of large commits (verified)\n`src/reviews.ts:96-113` (`readReviews`)\n\nThe new reader cap counts the whole JSON line. That line includes the review's `lines` stamp, which holds one 16-hex key per changed line in the commit. The writer has no matching limit: `reviews --record` in `src/commands/reviews.ts` writes a line of any size. A commit with about 14,000 or more changed lines (a lockfile bump, generated code, a rewritten file) therefore gets a legitimate review that is recorded and then hidden from every reader.\n\n- **What I ran:** in a scratch repo, I rewrote an 8,000-line file (16,000 changed lines), ran `git gloss add`, then `reviews --stamp`, then `reviews --record` with a short \"VERDICT: CLEAN\" report. The note line came to 304,224 bytes.\n- **With this change:** `git gloss reviews HEAD` prints `warning: skipped 1 review record … longer than 256 KiB` and `no reviews recorded`. `show` says `reviews: none recorded`.\n- **With the pre-change build** (a worktree at HEAD~1), the same commit shows `bugs: no defects found (a, 2026-10-24)`.\n\nDecisions on that review are dropped too, because `readDecisions` only keeps decisions whose review id `readReviews` returned. So the tool records reviews that it then refuses to read, and the bigger the commit, the more likely it is to look unreviewed.\n\n**Fix options:** cap the `findings` text instead of the whole line, or scale the cap with the stamp's size. Either way, enforce the same limit in `--record`.\n\n### 2. Minor: cleaning gloss text in `readNote` changes the review stamps (verified)\n`src/git.ts:259` (`cleanStrings` → `plain(v, true)`)\n\n`stamp()` and `reviewStatus()` hash the group text through `readNote` (`groupKey` in `src/reviews.ts:66`). `plain` turns `\\r` into a space, and also changes C1 controls, U+2028/2029 and bidi marks. So any gloss with that text now gets different group keys.\n\n- **What I ran:** a gloss whose intent is `\"line one\\r\\nline two\"`. The old build's `reviews --stamp` gives group key `91d6516161f76f1e`; the new build gives `cbaddec02c8b50d6`.\n- **Outcome:** reviews recorded before the upgrade on such glosses now show \"gloss changed since review\". A reviewer that stamps with a different git-gloss build (git-gud bundles its own copy) will disagree with readers on this build.\n- **Also:** `add` still stores the raw text. Readers, and anything that `carry` or `adopt` writes, see the cleaned text, so the stored and read versions of one gloss differ.\n\n### 3. Minor: `why` still prints commit subjects raw to the terminal (verified)\n`src/commands/why.ts:183`\n\nThe TODO's threat model covers \"commit text\" and lists `why` as fixed, but `s.subject` is printed without `plain()`.\n\n- **What I ran:** a commit whose subject is `subj\\e]52;c;cHduZWQ=\\a\\e[2J`, then `git gloss why f:1`.\n- **Outcome:** the output contains `^[]52;c;cHduZWQ=^G^[[2J`, a clipboard write followed by a screen clear.\n\n`show`, `hunks` and `reviews` printed no ESC byte for the same commit. The test \"escape sequences\" only puts escapes in note text and file names, never in the commit subject, so it misses this.\n\nI found nothing else of substance. The ary request-target fix, the sync check on the fetched notes ref, the group and list caps, the claim budget, the bounded markdown scans and the prototype-free lookups all behaved as described when I checked them.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
