 **Unannounced deletion in `audit-todo` (h1). Read.** The diff removes the Release item \"Bump the git-gloss dependency in git-gud (it bundles 0.2.0 and cannot read format-4 glosses, so `git gud review` refuses them)\". The intent says only \"record each boundary result; drop done items\", and nothing in the audit shows that item done. It is a release blocker for the user's downstream tool, so this is an unrelated deletion that needs its own justification or needs to be restored.\n- **`audit-todo` also drops the \"do these before the next release\" heading** and rewrites it as an audit section. That is consistent with the intent, but it is worth noticing together with the item above.\n- **`proto-free-maps` `fileCount` (h18/h19 area). Read.** The `fileCount` change is a real fix, since `seen['__proto__'] = true` is silently ignored on a plain object. It is correct, but it is a different defect from the read-mark one the intent describes.\n\n### 2. Misgraded necessity\n\n- **`terminal-clean` [required] bundles three different things.** (a) The `plain()` move into `plain.ts` and the import reshuffles across h4, h5, h13, h14, h15, h26, h30. These are mechanical, and the group should say so or be split out. (b) The `cleanStrings` change in `readNote`, which is the real fix. (c) Print-site `plain()` calls in `signals`, `why`, `hunkLabel` and `wrap`. Group (c) is needed for file names, but `wrap()` (h27) runs `plain()` over text that `readNote` has already cleaned. That call is redundant unless `wrap` also receives unclean review text. **Read.**\n- **`audit-tests` [supporting] is correct as supporting, but it contains guard tests for non-fixes** (ary Host checks, html default path, deep nesting, `__proto__` keys). Those are the author's \"defensive\" boundary guards, not fixes. The grouping is fine because they serve the task, but the intent line understates them.\n- **The `audit-tests` browser tests use `skip: skipBrowse`.** In an environment without a browser, the markdown-DoS and DOM-injection evidence is silently skipped. The intent does not mention this.\n\n### 3. Removal candidates\n\n- **`wrap()`'s `plain()` (h27)** is probably removable if every caller passes gloss text, which is already cleaned. Expected impact is nil for gloss text. If a caller passes review text, keep it. **Read**, not checked against every call site.\n- The `MAX_ITEMS` cap on `reasons` and `alternatives` is defensible. The `Group.hunks` cap is the one that needs care (see missing risks).\n\n### 4. Simplifications\n\n- **Markdown:** the `[` link regex now uses `s.slice(i, i + MAX_SPAN)` plus two inner length bounds. That is fine. The bold branch's `indexOf('**', i + 2)` is unbounded, but I could not construct a quadratic input for it, so I am not flagging it.\n- **`readReviews` (review-cap):** the `else if (!isDecisionLine(l)) long++` branch silently drops a long decision-shaped line with no count and no warning. Decision lines are read elsewhere with their own cap, so this is probably correct. A one-line comment saying so would help.\n\n### 5. Missing risks\n\n- **Fail-open verdict (the author lists it, but it is the most serious one).** A dropped over-cap review can remove the one record that says \"issues\", so a stage can read as clean or unreviewed. The warning goes only to stderr, which a reader may not see. A stage-level \"unreadable review, N records skipped\" marker in the reviews view would close this gap. **Read.**\n- **`MAX_ITEMS = 500` on `Group.hunks`.** A legitimate group that covers a generated-file or mass-rename commit can list more than 500 hunk references. Such a gloss is now refused whole, so `show` and `html` over a range fail. The author's \"real glosses have a few dozen groups\" justification covers groups, not hunks. **Read.**\n- **Refusal fails whole ranges.** The author already lists this, and the TODO follow-up covers it. It is a usability regression for any range that contains one bad gloss. Consider a per-commit warning.\n- **`sync` check only validates `^{commit}`.** A commit whose tree is not a notes tree still passes. The author lists this. Consider checking that the commit's tree looks like a notes tree (for example, `git ls-tree` yields only hex-named entries).\n- **Cleaned annotations are written back.** `adopt` and `carry` copy the cleaned text, which changes the stored note's content. This is disclosed, but it means a note read by an older version and rewritten now differs from the original. Worth a test.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
